You also brought us absolutely gorgeous floral arrangements.

79
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.700
Well, you deserve them.

80
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.360
They're so pretty.

81
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.500
And we have a little something for you because you're our very first guest on Office Lady

82
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.440
6.0.

83
00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:36.060
Thank you so much.

84
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:36.740
This is for you.

85
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:37.540
Thank you.

86
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:38.260
Can't wait.

87
00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.940
I've been looking at it to see what it is.

88
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.660
Well, the folks at Odyssey helped us make these for our guests moving forward, and we're

89
00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:46.420
so tickled.

90
00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.340
This is fantastic.

91
00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.580
Okay, turn it around.

92
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.100
Turn it around.

93
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:51.900
Oh, my gosh.

94
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:52.360
Oh.

95
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.800
Tell people what it says.

96
00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.800
World's best podcast gueSt.

97
00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:58.360
Woo-hoo!

98
00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.300
That's fantastic.

99
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.180
It's a mug.

100
00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.360
And who drew the cartoon?

101
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.960
Our friend Ileana.

102
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:03.760
It's amazing.

103
00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:04.060
Yeah.

104
00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.040
She's a great artiSt.

105
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:05.420
Yeah.

106
00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.800
I remember fondly saving a cross from Steve when he was testing for the pilot, and he

107
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.920
had the world's best boss mug holding it, and he was reading his monologue audition.

108
00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.200
And he was holding this, so very good.

109
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:17.780
Yay!

110
00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:24.540
Well, Allison, we told our fans we were going to be interviewing you, and they sent in a ton

111
00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.500
of questions, and I thought we could kick things off with this one.

112
00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.940
This is from Chelsea Frazier in Greenville, South Carolina.

113
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.980
Chelsea asked, did you go to college?

114
00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.960
And if so, what did you study?

115
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.140
I'd imagine that casting would take someone who's good with people, like maybe sociology

116
00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.220
or communications.

117
00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:49.740
But I'm curious, where Allison built that amazing ability to spot a good fit?

118
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.540
Oh, God bless you, Chelsea.

119
00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:52.460
That's a good question.

120
00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:56.320
I did indeed go to college, not for any kind of media anything.

121
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:02.620
I grew up back east, and I went to college in Los Angeles at Pomona College, and I was

122
00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.220
an art major and a math minor.

123
00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:10.740
After that, I went to business school, of all places, at UCLA, got an MBA degree, went

124
00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.400
to work in advertising for a year in New York City.

125
00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:14.840
I didn't like it.

126
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:15.420
I was miserable.

127
00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:20.880
And a friend of mine from business school, Lydia Woodward, who is a very successful writer-producer,

128
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.060
she wrote ER, et cetera, China Beach, a lot of shows.

129
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:25.820
She said, I'm at AFI.

130
00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:26.600
It's a gas.

131
00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:27.780
You should come out and do it.

132
00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.680
You can get good loans, student loans.

133
00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.860
So I did that, and I went to the American Film Institute.

134
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.940
And after AFI, I needed a job.

135
00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.780
And my same friend, Lydia, said, I think you'd like casting, because you remember faces.

136
00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.600
So I just applied for, that was when you had to type letters to try to get an interview

137
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:48.620
and you had to follow up the letter with a phone call.

138
00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.040
Anyway, one person called me back and I got the job with a casting director named Judith

139
00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.480
Wiener, the late Judith Wiener, who at the time was doing a lot of comedy for Thomas Harris.

140
00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.620
And this is the mid-80s.

141
00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.660
And so I started doing a lot of comedy then.

142
00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.020
I started doing a lot of comedy.

143
00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:09.520
My own taste was definitely developed just by having a bunch of brothers and sisters

144
00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.340
and my own vibe was developed.

145
00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.820
How many brothers and sisters do you have?

146
00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:15.560
I have five.

147
00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:17.660
So you're one of six?

148
00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.560
I'm one of six.

149
00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.600
And we were all into things like in the 60s and 70s, we worshipped the Three Stooges.

150
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:26.560
We worshipped Rona Martin's Laugh-In.

151
00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.900
We worshipped a lot of comedy back then.

152
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.900
Peter Sellers, Jack Lemmon, Walter Matthau.

153
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:36.940
Anyway, that was definitely developed for me by my older siblings, and we all were big comedy fans.

154
00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:40.240
You know, Allison, my dad would enter a room, and he would go,

155
00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:40.740
Enter!

156
00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:41.680
Oh.

157
00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.360
It was like Walter Matthau.

158
00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.220
And I didn't know what he was doing until years later.

159
00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:46.860
Oh, my God.

160
00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:47.720
And then I watched it with him.

161
00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:52.960
Yeah, we could quote lines from the odd couple in the party, Peter Sellers, forever and ever.

162
00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.580
Well, this next question is one that we ask all of our guests, which is,

163
00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.040
how did you get your job on The Office?

164
00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:00.200
Yes.

165
00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:01.400
Okay.

166
00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:14.240
I think I had just finished doing a show called Freaks and Geeks, and I think Greg Daniels liked the realness of the actors that we hired on Freaks and Geeks,

167
00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.160
which was the first time I had ever been asked to cast a show like that.

168
00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:22.040
It was kind of, when you look back, it was pretty earth-shattering to cast teenagers who were not like, you know.

169
00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.020
Then we called them WB people.

170
00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:27.520
The WB, the gorgeous kids who were 28 playing 18.

171
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:28.060
Mm-hmm.

172
00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.140
Because everyone had to be super pretty.

173
00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.860
Everyone had to be Dawson's Creek, and they were all in their mid-20s playing, you know, 17, 18.

174
00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.800
But Paul Feig and Judd Apatow were like, we just want real kids.

175
00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:42.940
And I understood immediately having been one of those myself.

176
00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:48.580
Then they were making a lot more teen shows than they do now, so having pretty well-versed in the teen stuff.

177
00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.080
It was the kids who didn't get the show I did right before that called Roswell High,

178
00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.160
who I brought in for Freaks and Geeks who I remembered were so good.

179
00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:58.720
And they came in, like James Franco, Jason Siegel.

180
00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:00.800
They came in, and they were right for Freaks and Geeks.

181
00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.360
Jill Greenberg, my colleague in New York, and Corinne Mares, my colleague in Vancouver,

182
00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:09.700
equally major in doing the pilot of Freaks and Geeks with getting the real kids.

183
00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:12.420
Seth Rogen and Jill Greenberg found Sam Levine.

184
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.440
But anyway, Greg, I think, just liked the way that was caSt.

185
00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.980
And so he called me in, and I interviewed.

186
00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:23.240
And I was so nervous because HBO had just started airing the British office.

187
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:26.320
At the same time, they had also started airing LEG.

188
00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:32.440
So it was like, what is this brand new, unbelievably smart, funny way they're doing comedy in Britain?

189
00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:33.180
It's incredible.

190
00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.820
So anyway, I was so excited to get an interview for the office.

191
00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:38.800
I'm sure he interviewed quite a few people.

192
00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:39.940
Greg is thorough.

193
00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.760
We had a good talk, and Greg was, you know, he's very thoughtful about his process.

194
00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.040
Yes, and I appreciate that.

195
00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:48.980
But I got that with Greg.

196
00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:52.960
I think there were probably a handful of casting people who interviewed for it,

197
00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.440
as we still have to interview for all of our jobs, like actors.

198
00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:56.820
And we do the same things.

199
00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.320
We beat ourselves up after an interview.

200
00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:59.420
God, why did I say that?

201
00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.040
That was stupid.

202
00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:02.640
Allison, with your resume, you still interview?

203
00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.940
They don't look at everything you've done and just say, give me Allison Jones, please?

204
00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.880
Not always, believe it or not.

205
00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:09.360
I know.

206
00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:09.860
I know.

207
00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:10.200
I agree.

208
00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.500
For half hours, I still have to sometimes, which is sad to me.

209
00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:21.720
You know, I got a call to play the bitch on a show recently, and they wanted me to come

210
00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:22.180
in and read.

211
00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:23.100
And I always do.

212
00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:23.840
I always do.

213
00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:27.400
I was traveling, and I was like going to miss the window to audition.

214
00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.220
And I said, you know, if they want to see me play a bitch, ask them to watch the first

215
00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:32.700
nine years of The Office.

216
00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:32.920
Yes.

217
00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.700
Like, just ask them to watch nine seasons, any episode.

218
00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:37.220
Yeah, yeah.

219
00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:38.540
And they'll find the bitch.

220
00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:39.240
Wow.

221
00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:40.640
I'm sorry about that.

222
00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:41.300
That's okay.

223
00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.940
An excruciating part of my job is having to have people come in who should never have

224
00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:46.500
to audition.

225
00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:53.800
And still, people, young producers, young directors, young studio people, young everything, have

226
00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.700
to see people read because they don't have imagination.

227
00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:02.280
Like, I have a fine-tuned imagination about taking someone, their personality, and seeing

228
00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:09.360
if it can be transferred into an acting thing, which is a large part, I will say, of how the

229
00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.460
Judd Apatow, Paul Feig Office School of Comedy came along.

230
00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:19.800
Because we have to look beyond how people do line readings and see what they're like as

231
00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:20.140
people.

232
00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.800
Again, people like Judd and huge comedy geniuses who sort of started this whole real comedy

233
00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:28.260
kind of vibe or improv-y kind of comedy vibe.

234
00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:31.720
But they'll see what they can bring to it as opposed to see how they read the lines

235
00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:32.380
that you wrote.

236
00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:33.580
Yeah.

237
00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.080
It doesn't have to be word for word.

238
00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:41.960
And people who are funnier than anything that's written on the page get a chance to improvise

239
00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:46.420
like Steve Carell and some of the early Second City people who came to town when I first met

240
00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:50.680
them, Nia Vardalos and Steve Carell, people like that who were Second City back in the early

241
00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:56.220
90s probably, would come in and bless them all, not be the greatest auditioners.

242
00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:00.760
Because comedy doesn't always translate well into auditioning with somebody else's sitcom

243
00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.800
jokes and punching the lines up.

244
00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:08.140
And back in the 90s, if you didn't stay on book, as they say, I think producers were horrified.

245
00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:09.440
No, she changed that line.

246
00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:10.160
I can't hire them.

247
00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:18.300
But with the onslaught of a lot of improv comedy from stand-up comedy to improv comedy

248
00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:18.780
casting.

249
00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.520
One of the sad things is having to have people read who absolutely don't need to read.

250
00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.100
Well, I normally don't.

251
00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:26.140
We all feel that way.

252
00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:29.420
I guarantee you all casting people have great empathy for actors.

253
00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.520
Or believe me, we wouldn't be doing this.

254
00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:36.540
I talk to aspiring actors sometimes, go into their classes and take questions.

255
00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:41.680
And one of the things that I always really try to tell people is that the casting director

256
00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:42.820
is rooting for you.

257
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:47.020
They want nothing more than for you to come in and be great.

258
00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:47.400
Yeah.

259
00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:54.220
Because if you're great, their job is done and they have found you and it's exciting to

260
00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:54.380
them.

261
00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.880
So they are absolutely rooting for you.

262
00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:00.420
And I say like, it's okay to ask a question.

263
00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.880
Let them help you get this job.

264
00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:03.120
Yeah.

265
00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:03.660
Yeah.

266
00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:04.340
That's perfect.

267
00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:05.080
That's perfect.

268
00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:09.940
I mean, now it's still a little bit different because now I think the trend has headed to

269
00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:10.940
a lot of self-taping.

270
00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:11.280
Yeah.

271
00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:11.740
Oh, yeah.

272
00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:18.380
Which for me, self-taping is better for like a lot of pre-reads when I can meet infinitely

273
00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.700
more people with pre-tape.

274
00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:22.800
We should tell people what a pre-tape is.

275
00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:27.480
That's where if you get an audition for a show, they email you the materials and then

276
00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:33.440
in your house, you put yourself on tape reading the scene and then you send it back to the

277
00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.080
casting director.

278
00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.640
But back when we were auditioning, it was more common that you would have to physically

279
00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:43.520
go into the casting office and sit across from someone like Phyllis or someone like

280
00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:46.160
you and you read the scene with us.

281
00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:46.440
Yes.

282
00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:49.920
And we could even get maybe a little notes or feedback in the moment.

283
00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:56.300
But now it really is a lot more since COVID, especially you send in a tape of yourself.

284
00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:58.480
I think it's less stressful.

285
00:13:58.740 --> 00:13:59.480
I think so too.

286
00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:02.180
And I think actors, we now give people the choice.

287
00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:07.900
They'll often pick, I don't want to drive across town at two o'clock and I can do 10 takes

288
00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:08.280
at home.

289
00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.060
Those are considered pre-reads.

290
00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:14.760
I mean, and then they go to the producers and then we still have auditions with producers.

291
00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:16.900
But I think it's less in person.

292
00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:17.500
Oh, for sure.

293
00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:18.520
We couldn't for a while.

294
00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:23.200
And that was, you know, it was, I'll be honest, it was fairly hurtful when during COVID

295
00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:28.320
people would accuse casting people of not doing our jobs because we were having people self-tape.

296
00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:29.860
That was massively hurtful.

297
00:14:29.920 --> 00:14:30.140
Yeah.

298
00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:34.200
Because as a rule, who goes to bat more for an actor than a casting person?

299
00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:34.560
Exactly.

300
00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:35.240
Nobody does.

301
00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:35.780
Exactly.

302
00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:40.480
So many people out there have gigantic parts and careers and they have no idea how close

303
00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:44.700
they came to not getting that job until a casting director fought for them and showed

304
00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:48.540
them every piece of tape we could find, showed them being interviewed on every talk show,

305
00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:52.040
everything, or didn't let them take another job and made sure they stayed available for

306
00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.100
the pilot of the office kind of thing.

307
00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:54.860
That was rough.

308
00:14:54.980 --> 00:15:02.700
But what came of that is that all the self-taping, what came of it was we can put so many more

309
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:06.800
people on tape everywhere, you know, Britain, everywhere, and they get shots at it.

310
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.960
Yeah, because they're not having to come into the room.

311
00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:10.820
So you can audition wherever you are in the world.

312
00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:13.440
Well, for people we don't know, it just opens it up.

313
00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.740
It opens up the floodgates and we can see if we're casting kids, they can go home and do

314
00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:22.960
it with their folks and we are trained to spot something on the video.

315
00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:25.220
We don't necessarily go by line readings.

316
00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:25.660
Yeah.

317
00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:28.780
We leave that to the executive producers and then we argue with that.

318
00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:30.560
Guys, look at this person.

319
00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:31.540
This kid is fantastic.

320
00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:34.140
He's green, but he can, you know, that's what you get.

321
00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:38.180
You get, it's a leap of faith and you take someone green who you like their vibe and you

322
00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:38.800
go with it.

323
00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.180
Boy, that has worked for me in comedy.

324
00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:41.880
That's for sure.

325
00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:42.740
Absolutely.

326
00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:43.440
Yeah.

327
00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.220
Well, I love not having to sit in the hallway anymore.

328
00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:50.280
The long hallway where you're waiting to go in and there's like eight people that look

329
00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:51.460
almost just like you.

330
00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.280
And then you can hear the audition.

331
00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:57.760
You can hear the person before you reading the same lines you're about to read.

332
00:15:58.080 --> 00:15:58.920
Am I crazy?

333
00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.040
I like all of that.

334
00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:00.980
No, it's okay.

335
00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:03.080
You like the hallway?

336
00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.060
A little bit.

337
00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:06.940
What do you like about the hallway?

338
00:16:06.940 --> 00:16:07.720
You wouldn't anymore.

339
00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:08.780
I wouldn't?

340
00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:09.620
I don't think so.

341
00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:13.220
Maybe when you're younger and you really have to audition and go for something, you like

342
00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:14.820
that hallway because there's the camaraderie.

343
00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:20.880
What I always admire is the camaraderie of actors seeing each other at auditions and they're

344
00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:24.380
friendly and they haven't seen someone in a while and they're saying, hey, and they're

345
00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:26.680
really thinking, oh, I'm up against this person.

346
00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:30.400
I'm up against this person again and again because a lot of actors know who they're up

347
00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:30.640
againSt.

348
00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.900
They see the sign in sheet and they know who just came in.

349
00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:36.980
My biggest competition my whole career was always Alison Hannigan.

350
00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:37.880
I'm sure.

351
00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.460
And Mary Lynn Ricegup was my other one.

352
00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:47.780
I bet.

353
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:48.760
In fact, she was.

354
00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:50.600
She was up for Pam.

355
00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:51.600
She was.

356
00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:53.540
And she was really good, too.

357
00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:54.840
I know.

358
00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:55.360
I know.

359
00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:58.460
She and I tested against each other many a time.

360
00:16:58.560 --> 00:16:59.420
Yes, I'm sure you did.

361
00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:05.680
Mary Lynn has a very funny bit that she does in her stand up where people ask for her autograph

362
00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:07.680
and they say you were great as Pam.

363
00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:12.400
And she just was like, OK, she just like she just doesn't even react anymore.

364
00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:13.460
That's very funny.

365
00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:17.880
I have a very funny Mary Lynn Ricegup story, which is that I had been auditioning for this

366
00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.880
pilot for the Dan Band.

367
00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:23.420
So he was doing the show and there was the part of the waitress.

368
00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:24.440
It was a lead girl.

369
00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:30.060
And when I read the sides, I thought, oh, I'm going to do like my best Mary Lynn Ricegup.

370
00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:35.540
That's that is I'm literally going to do an impression of her because that's how I see

371
00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:36.060
this part.

372
00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:39.980
And I walked into the test audition and she was sitting right there.

373
00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:44.020
And I thought, you know what, at least I knew what they were going for.

374
00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.080
Like, I at least interpreted it right.

375
00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:47.260
And then guess what?

376
00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:48.180
There was one other girl.

377
00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.520
There was tall, blonde Australian actress.

378
00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:52.020
She got the part.

379
00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:52.800
Oh, my God.

380
00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:54.360
Mary Lynn and I did not get it.

381
00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:55.860
It went to the third girl.

382
00:17:56.540 --> 00:17:58.160
But I thought that was really funny.

383
00:17:58.340 --> 00:17:58.460
Yeah.

384
00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:00.300
I was like, oh, there she is.

385
00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:00.940
Yeah.

386
00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:03.080
Mine is Rachel Harris.

387
00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:03.800
Oh, sure.

388
00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:04.240
Of course.

389
00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:08.580
And then she ended up playing my sister in the finale, which is perfect.

390
00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:08.820
The beSt.

391
00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:12.460
But I feel like there's a show out there somewhere where we're related.

392
00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:13.000
Yes.

393
00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:15.660
The Groundlings was very, very good to the office.

394
00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:18.160
Everybody in the Groundlings and UCB and all of that.

395
00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:18.610
Yeah.

396
00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:24.880
Well, speaking of that, a lot of people wrote in to ask, how do you start casting a project?

397
00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:31.300
Can you tell us a little bit about how you assemble a cast and what were some of the things

398
00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:34.340
that you and Greg first talked about in terms of a vision for the show?

399
00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:40.180
Well, we had a template, the British office, and I think we discussed – it didn't make

400
00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:44.300
sense to me to really do duplicates of the people in the British office, but their kind

401
00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:45.020
of essence.

402
00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:48.420
You know, Jim was a little less self-confident, that kind of thing.

403
00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:51.580
And so I think he mostly discussed just real people.

404
00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:54.500
He just – we just need to get real people in here to start to audition.

405
00:18:54.500 --> 00:19:00.040
So I started by doing a lot of pre-reads, meaning they came to my office and would read

406
00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:03.960
the sides, and I would – at the time, I don't think we taped pre-reads.

407
00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:05.380
We could ask Phyllis that question.

408
00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:06.220
I don't think we did.

409
00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:12.740
And that – we were in an office across from CBS Radford, and it was just Phyllis and

410
00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:14.700
me, the before times Phyllis.

411
00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:15.700
Yes, that's right.

412
00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:16.060
Yeah.

413
00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:19.800
And we always pre-read.

414
00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:21.760
Every casting director, you do so much legwork.

415
00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:23.580
You're pre-reading so many actors, blah, blah, blah.

416
00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:27.300
I knew Jenna, I knew you, because you had already done Undeclared, right?

417
00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:27.880
You had done –

418
00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:28.320
Yeah, well –

419
00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:29.580
Yeah, and I just knew you because of Naomi.

420
00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.080
Yeah, you took a general meeting with me.

421
00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:32.760
Yes, that's right.

422
00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:33.700
Was Phyllis there?

423
00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:34.620
Phyllis was there.

424
00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:35.500
St. Louis, yeah.

425
00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:35.920
Right.

426
00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:40.680
And then you called me in for a role on Undeclared.

427
00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:41.420
Right, right.

428
00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:42.920
And I got it.

429
00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:43.220
Right.

430
00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:44.180
It was one line.

431
00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:44.460
Yep.

432
00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:48.540
And then you called me back all the time for different shows.

433
00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:51.560
And I never got any jobs.

434
00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:55.740
But for five years, you kept bringing me into your office, Allison.

435
00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.400
Didn't you do Spin City?

436
00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:00.400
Spin City was my very first job.

437
00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:02.980
That was the very first one.

438
00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:04.780
And then Undeclared.

439
00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:05.480
Okay, great.

440
00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.280
You had brought me in for Freaks and Geeks.

441
00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:09.800
Oh, right.

442
00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:10.140
Okay.

443
00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:11.120
But I didn't go anywhere.

444
00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:11.660
Okay.

445
00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:16.380
But you just kept calling me back and calling me back and calling me back for auditions.

446
00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:23.260
And then for the office, you called Naomi and you said, I'm going to bring Jenna straight to producers.

447
00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:27.180
I think she would be great for this role of Pam, but you need to tell her something.

448
00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:29.780
Tell her two things.

449
00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:34.680
Dare to bore me with your audition.

450
00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:37.440
I'll never forget those words.

451
00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:39.520
Dare to bore me.

452
00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:43.500
Do not come in and do a bunch of comedy shtick.

453
00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:47.820
She needs to play it super real and also no glam.

454
00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.460
I want her coming in looking natural.

455
00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:50.920
Right.

456
00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:52.860
That's what they're looking for.

457
00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:54.680
I was like, got it.

458
00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.880
Well, you came to mind immediately, for sure.

459
00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.420
Jenna Fisher for that part.

460
00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:03.180
And also, those were the days where you didn't have to attach big names to anything.

461
00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:09.060
Greg was very open just to new faces and real people.

462
00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.160
And I knew what he meant right away.

463
00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:14.840
So I just went back and I would usually do a week or two of pre-reads, getting to know everybody.

464
00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:19.220
And I would call Naomi and we would get all the groundlings in and et cetera, et cetera.

465
00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:27.800
And I did not want to duplicate the people on the British show, but just their sort of their essence in there, what they would bring to a similar character.

466
00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:36.120
I think the pilot of the office had a bit of a mandate from NBC to do it like the pilot of the British office.

467
00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:36.620
Yes.

468
00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:37.900
It was scripted the same.

469
00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:42.600
With all due respect to Greg, it bugged me that we had some Britishisms in the script.

470
00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:44.400
Like, you like a drink after work?

471
00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:45.360
I'll never forget that.

472
00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:46.640
That's such not an American.

473
00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:47.820
Way to say that.

474
00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:48.100
American.

475
00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:48.540
Yeah.

476
00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:49.540
Way to say that.

477
00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:50.220
That was Jim.

478
00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:52.440
All the Jims had to say, so you like a drink after work?

479
00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:54.920
And I was like, oh, Americans don't say that.

480
00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:55.660
Please change it.

481
00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:58.340
But I don't think it was changed, at least for the auditions.

482
00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:00.300
So it's a couple of weeks of pre-reading.

483
00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:04.000
I have all the lists of who came in to pre-read here if you ever want to look at them.

484
00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:05.140
I kind of.

485
00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:10.680
Oh, including, you know, so many people now who are huge and who then went on to read for producers but didn't get the job.

486
00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:16.540
Allison, you gave me, when the show wrapped as a gift, my sign-in sheet the day I auditioned.

487
00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:17.700
And I still have it.

488
00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:17.960
Oh, good.

489
00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:18.340
Yeah.

490
00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:21.480
I even kept it in the red folder you gave it to me.

491
00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:21.720
Oh.

492
00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:26.140
And reading the same day that I was reading was Catherine Hahn.

493
00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:27.300
Oh, my gosh.

494
00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:27.740
Yeah.

495
00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:29.360
See, that stuff is great about casting.

496
00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:35.580
Many colleagues and I have those old sign-in sheets and the old sheets with notes about what we said about actors and things.

497
00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:38.700
At the time, everybody who was anybody in comedy came in.

498
00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:42.620
A couple people passed or had deals at CBS or something and couldn't come in.

499
00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:44.080
But everybody wanted to do the show.

500
00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:46.400
So we had a lot of great producer sessions.

501
00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:49.100
I remember my producer session with you.

502
00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:49.780
I'm sure you do.

503
00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:51.540
You were in the stellar first day.

504
00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:52.680
Yeah.

505
00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:56.120
You said that you found half the caSt.

506
00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:56.780
The first day.

507
00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:58.020
The first day of producer sessions.

508
00:22:58.020 --> 00:22:59.160
That usually happens, by the way.

509
00:22:59.360 --> 00:22:59.980
Is that right?

510
00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:00.400
Uh-huh.

511
00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:01.260
That's very common.

512
00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:03.100
Casting people bring in their favorite people firSt.

513
00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:07.460
And if you get them on the day they're available, there are so many independent variables in casting.

514
00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:10.300
We're at mercy of other people scheduling, blah, blah, blah, blah.

515
00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:15.140
But frequently for a movie and pilots, et cetera, you cast it the first week.

516
00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:20.320
But because you have to go through the steps and people don't know and they can't think and they can't decide.

517
00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:22.600
And there's 8,000 people who have to agree on one person.

518
00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:25.600
Now, anyway, more than was then, for sure.

519
00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:27.380
Well, I remember coming in.

520
00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:27.840
Yeah.

521
00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:29.880
And you told me that—

522
00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:30.380
Big table.

523
00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:31.100
Yeah.

524
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:31.480
Yes.

525
00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:38.920
At the producer's session.

526
00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:39.240
Right.

527
00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:44.940
I'm just going to ask you some questions and then you answer how you think Pam would answer the question.

528
00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:51.220
And I remember thinking, okay, Allison's note was, dare to bore me.

529
00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:53.040
So, okay.

530
00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:54.920
So, I'm sitting there.

531
00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:56.340
The camera is on me.

532
00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:57.880
There's all these people watching me.

533
00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:01.860
And you say, so, Pam, do you like being a receptionist?

534
00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:04.520
And I went like this.

535
00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:07.840
No.

536
00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.160
And then I didn't say anything else.

537
00:24:12.160 --> 00:24:14.260
And the room was like crickets.

538
00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:15.900
And my heart was pounding.

539
00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:16.920
I'm like sweating.

540
00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:18.980
And then everybody burst into laughter.

541
00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:22.100
And I was like, yes, that's what I was going for.

542
00:24:22.100 --> 00:24:31.080
Because I thought, listen, this gal, my circumstances are that my crazy boss has brought me in front of a documentary crew.

543
00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:32.420
I don't have media training.

544
00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.020
I don't know how to give a good interview.

545
00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:37.920
I don't want to say anything bad.

546
00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:39.960
But I also don't want to lie.

547
00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:44.820
So, I'm going to just say no and leave it at that.

548
00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:57.040
And I thought the more interesting thing would be all the things you see me not say than anything I could possibly say.

549
00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:00.240
Because I'm not a clever improviser with lines.

550
00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:02.660
I am not a Zach Woods.

551
00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:05.300
I do not have that command of language.

552
00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:09.740
But I'm like, but perhaps there's much I could say without saying much at all.

553
00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:16.740
I would say, though, Jenna, you're an excellent emotional improviser because you have great reactions and you react very honestly.

554
00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:18.920
That's another form of improv.

555
00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:20.120
Well, thank you.

556
00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:23.620
I think of myself as not a very good improviser, actually.

557
00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:24.400
I disagree.

558
00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:25.060
I disagree.

559
00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:28.360
I feel like I'm a good reactor to improvising, I guess.

560
00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:29.740
But that's what The Office was all about.

561
00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:32.360
That style was reacting as opposed to acting.

562
00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:36.820
But I remember you giving me a little wink after that moment.

563
00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:37.300
Oh, I probably did.

564
00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:39.680
Talk about, like, encouraging the actor.

565
00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:41.120
And I was like, okay, good.

566
00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:46.860
And I wanted to do right by you because you had believed in me for all those years.

567
00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:50.860
I wanted to pay that back by doing a good job.

568
00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:51.240
Yeah.

569
00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:53.380
Oh, I remember them all so vividly.

570
00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:53.720
I do.

571
00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:55.880
And I would read, Phyllis did the taping.

572
00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:57.920
Those were the days where it was VHS tape.

573
00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:04.220
And we had to transfer the VHS tapes in real time and make, like, six copies of each VHS tape.

574
00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:05.560
Oh, my gosh.

575
00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:12.700
And then send them to NBC or send them to – we had to overnight them to England, to Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

576
00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:19.400
And you'd play, record, and you'd have to record for 90 minutes while you go through all the auditions.

577
00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:21.480
Kids don't have to do that these days.

578
00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:23.800
They just – and something is digitally recorded.

579
00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:25.980
I remember being so nervous.

580
00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:26.380
I bet.

581
00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:29.180
Knowing that you were sending our tapes to Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

582
00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:30.640
I probably should never have said anything.

583
00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:31.160
I know.

584
00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:36.020
Well, I think my agent might have told me that, you know, you called them.

585
00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:36.780
You're like, any feedback?

586
00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:37.620
Any feedback?

587
00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:38.160
Yeah, yeah.

588
00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:41.780
And they said, well, they did send you to Ricky and Stephen.

589
00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:43.140
And I was like, ha, ha, ha.

590
00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:45.480
I'm like, well, that's good news.

591
00:26:45.620 --> 00:26:46.480
I'm really scared.

592
00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:51.460
I also remember right after your audition because you were really maybe one of the first four people to read.

593
00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:54.600
I don't have the exact number of people, but that was our first session.

594
00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:59.100
And Terry Weinberg, you did a long audition because we had a lot of pages then to audition.

595
00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:03.160
And then Terry said to you, I could watch you read all day long.

596
00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:04.160
I remember she said that.

597
00:27:04.240 --> 00:27:07.720
I think I even have that on tape, her saying that after your audition.

598
00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:09.540
So she picked you right off the bat.

599
00:27:10.200 --> 00:27:11.140
I love Terry.

600
00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:12.180
That's so great.

601
00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:14.480
But it was such a long process.

602
00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:19.200
But you were a no-brainer to bring in for the first day anyway.

603
00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:24.920
I do try to bring in who I think are the best for the first day so that the writers get encouraged, et cetera, et cetera.

604
00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:26.440
That's so sweet.

605
00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:27.900
I'm curious to ask you.

606
00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:34.680
You, on the British pilot, because I had thought that maybe Steve Carell never really watched the British pilot.

607
00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:35.240
Steve never did.

608
00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:36.260
Because he didn't want to imitate.

609
00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:37.380
He never did.

610
00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:38.600
He never did.

611
00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:39.980
And what about you guys?

612
00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:41.360
Oh, I loved it.

613
00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:42.020
You did.

614
00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:42.920
You were aware of it.

615
00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:43.580
I loved it.

616
00:27:43.660 --> 00:27:45.460
But I remember that.

617
00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:50.440
And I was like, wow, that's going to be huge and scary and daunting because it was done so well.

618
00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:51.580
And I was so worried.

619
00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.380
But I thought if anyone can do it, it'll be Greg.

620
00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:59.500
If anyone can, like, give it a really great, like, second go, it would be Greg.

621
00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:02.020
And I think NBC had just done coupling and it wasn't.

622
00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:02.900
It was not.

623
00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:03.240
It did not go.

624
00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:04.160
It was not a success.

625
00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:05.080
It hadn't translated.

626
00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:05.500
Yeah.

627
00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:09.920
And I remember getting the call about The Office and I was so excited.

628
00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:10.800
I had watched the whole thing.

629
00:28:10.840 --> 00:28:11.080
Yes.

630
00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:11.940
Oh, so you did.

631
00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:12.440
You did.

632
00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:14.900
And I thought, really?

633
00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:17.360
They're going to put that on American television?

634
00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:19.640
And then I was so worried.

635
00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:21.040
Oh, no.

636
00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:22.840
Are they going to change it?

637
00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:23.160
Yeah.

638
00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:23.500
Right.

639
00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:32.000
But as soon as I met Greg and really, as soon as I got the phone call from your office saying, dare to bore me, I was like, oh, they're doing it.

640
00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:33.400
They're really going to do it.

641
00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:34.540
They're going to take a big swing.

642
00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:35.580
And I was excited.

643
00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:36.060
Yeah.

644
00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:41.860
It was something I remember vividly the first meeting we had with the network when he had a list of names.

645
00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:44.140
And they were like, let's try Philip Seymour Hoffman.

646
00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:46.900
And then, you know, let's go for Paul Giamatti or whatever.

647
00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:48.240
And I'm so jaded.

648
00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:49.760
I'm like, they're never going to do.

649
00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:52.360
In those days, nobody did TV.

650
00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:55.040
TV was the – comedy was the bottom of the barrel.

651
00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:56.060
It's so true.

652
00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:57.800
It was the bottom of the barrel.

653
00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.600
To me, it was the top of the mountain.

654
00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:01.260
It's all I ever wanted to do.

655
00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:03.120
But for the business, bottom of the barrel.

656
00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:03.560
For sure.

657
00:29:03.700 --> 00:29:04.980
You're a comedy casting person.

658
00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:05.960
You're a comedy actor.

659
00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:07.220
You're a comedy writer.

660
00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:08.340
You're a comedy director.

661
00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:09.680
You couldn't get out of it.

662
00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:18.720
But anyway, this was the first time, other than Freaks and Geeks, I had the opportunity to get real people who didn't have to be, you know, vaudeville funny.

663
00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:19.080
Yep.

664
00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:22.600
Or sitcom funny, which is a very difficult thing to be.

665
00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:34.160
And so that just goes back into my past of the various kinds of comedy I have been able to witness being, you know, that's a whole other story.

666
00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:36.080
Comedy now being in a bit of a coma.

667
00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:40.080
But they wanted big names like Paul Giamatti.

668
00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:45.300
Well, they went – yes, right away they wanted to – that wasn't always the way it was at NBC anyway.

669
00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:47.580
Some networks always had to attach a big name.

670
00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:51.140
I recall CBS always wanted to attach a big name to get anything going.

671
00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:55.720
NBC was more into stand-ups at the time and having a deal with it.

672
00:29:55.720 --> 00:30:03.200
They always made deals with certain stand-up comedians and developed roles for – in fact,

673
00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:12.980
Steve Carell at the time was under contract to a show for one of the stand-ups they had done a deal with and did probably a nine-episode –

674
00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:13.980
Tom Papa.

675
00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:14.800
Tom Papa.

676
00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:15.090
Yeah.

677
00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:15.460
Come to Papa.

678
00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:17.040
He was on Tom Papa's show.

679
00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:21.840
And we had to wait for him to be released from that for NBC to even have a shot at getting Steve Carell.

680
00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:28.840
But Nancy Perkins, who was the head of casting at NBC Universal, always knew Steve would be a top choice for this.

681
00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:31.720
I think everybody pretty much knew Steve would be a top choice for this.

682
00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:38.500
But for the first month or so, we couldn't get him and it was like, oh, okay, we'll keep bringing people in and we brought in the best of the beSt.

683
00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:40.520
Everybody was a different version of Michael Scott.

684
00:30:40.720 --> 00:30:41.580
That could have worked.

685
00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:43.260
Patton Oswalt could have worked.

686
00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:45.100
Bob Odenkirk would have been amazing.

687
00:30:45.880 --> 00:30:49.420
And, you know, all the Dwights we saw could have been a different version of it.

688
00:30:49.460 --> 00:30:50.660
That happens in casting a lot.

689
00:30:50.720 --> 00:30:52.180
You do have a lot of versions, but –

690
00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:56.240
On the day I read, you know, I saw that –

691
00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:58.380
I remember you standing up there in front of the camera and everything.

692
00:30:58.380 --> 00:30:58.820
I do.

693
00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:04.480
Well, Eric Stonestreet read that day.

694
00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:05.100
Oh, okay.

695
00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:05.300
Good.

696
00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:05.600
Yeah.

697
00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:06.360
For Kevin.

698
00:31:06.680 --> 00:31:06.840
Yeah.

699
00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:07.860
Isn't that funny?

700
00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:08.420
That's amazing.

701
00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:09.640
I probably have proof of that.

702
00:31:09.780 --> 00:31:09.900
Yeah.

703
00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:10.160
Yeah.

704
00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:12.180
It was on my sign-in.

705
00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:15.720
A lot of people wanted to know who was the hardest role to caSt.

706
00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:16.820
Was it Michael Scott?

707
00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:25.560
I would say for me as a casting person, the hardest – no, we had choices for Michael Scott.

708
00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:26.880
For me, it wasn't the hardeSt.

709
00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:31.080
For me, it was finding a good Brian or a good –

710
00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:35.240
it was the supporting cast that we didn't quite know if they would be sustained through

711
00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:37.260
the whole show like they were in the British office.

712
00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:38.580
So filling out the world –

713
00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:38.720
But those were harder.

714
00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:43.500
Filling out the world was actually tougher because for smaller roles, as you probably

715
00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:48.740
know from your early days in auditioning, it's really hard to be totally real and interesting

716
00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:52.800
and fun and just say yes and no, it's enough or no and no, it's enough.

717
00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:54.700
Or yes, or you have one line.

718
00:31:55.160 --> 00:31:57.240
And your audition scene is one line.

719
00:31:57.440 --> 00:31:58.920
You're like, how do I stand out?

720
00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:01.760
How do I show that I can live in this world?

721
00:32:01.820 --> 00:32:01.870
It's so much tougher.

722
00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:02.760
It's really hard.

723
00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:06.860
It's even tougher for us because you need to see what can be sustained in the background

724
00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:11.180
compared to how amazing they were on the British office.

725
00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:15.860
Still, I think the best comedy ever made is the British office, if I can confess that.

726
00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:16.220
I do.

727
00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:17.960
Other than all the family.

728
00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:18.680
I know.

729
00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:19.360
Sorry.

730
00:32:19.580 --> 00:32:20.040
Hey, wait.

731
00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:21.580
It's Office 86.0.

732
00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:22.420
She can say that.

733
00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:25.960
Just because he invented it.

734
00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:31.900
But I would say that for me, the hardest was choosing all these amazing people from

735
00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:36.640
Groundlings and stuff and really bringing them forward and seeing what Greg could get

736
00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:38.120
out of them in terms of characters.

737
00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:44.160
I mean, the happiest day for me was when Ken Coapis came up to me and said, Alison, let

738
00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:45.920
Phyllis read with the actors.

739
00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:49.040
I want to see if she can do it.

740
00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:54.700
This was at the testing because I was reading with Steve Carell and stuff while they had

741
00:32:54.700 --> 00:32:56.920
the professional cameraman there taking it.

742
00:32:57.060 --> 00:32:59.660
And so Ken just came up to me and asked if I could let Phyllis read.

743
00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:00.880
I was like, yeah, that'd be great.

744
00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:02.520
And he said, maybe she could be in the background.

745
00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:04.520
I'm like, oh, my God.

746
00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:05.340
Yes.

