They go to an industry night and they see if they should sign certain people or whatever.

997
00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:22.060
We also have a network of comedy managers who need to get more credit than they ever get.

998
00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:23.840
I didn't discover anybody.

999
00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:27.620
The comedy manager discovered that person before I brought them in.

1000
00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:30.480
You know, Christy Smith discovered Charlene Yee.

1001
00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:32.400
Jimmy Miller discovered everybody.

1002
00:43:32.660 --> 00:43:34.620
Dave Becky, Dave Miner.

1003
00:43:34.760 --> 00:43:39.740
They discovered all these people and brought them up from nothing and encouraged them and

1004
00:43:39.740 --> 00:43:41.640
put together these shows with them.

1005
00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:45.160
And all the greats are with all these unbelievable comedy managers.

1006
00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:47.820
So I give them all the credit for doing the discovering.

1007
00:43:48.120 --> 00:43:49.900
Well, that's how I feel about Naomi.

1008
00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:50.580
Naomi.

1009
00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:51.140
I mean.

1010
00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:53.720
Everybody she signs ends up on SNL.

1011
00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:54.860
Kristen Wiig.

1012
00:43:54.900 --> 00:43:55.220
It's amazing.

1013
00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:56.060
Jerry Miner.

1014
00:43:56.140 --> 00:43:56.700
Yep, exactly.

1015
00:43:56.700 --> 00:44:02.780
So for whatever reason, whatever thing we have in our brains that makes us focus on,

1016
00:44:02.920 --> 00:44:05.420
I mean, we can visually and hear people in our brains.

1017
00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:06.800
Maybe we're all psychos.

1018
00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:07.180
I don't know.

1019
00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:12.660
But whatever that is, comedy managers need to have a lot more credit too.

1020
00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:13.480
Do they get credit?

1021
00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:14.020
I don't know.

1022
00:44:14.160 --> 00:44:14.780
I don't even know.

1023
00:44:14.900 --> 00:44:16.540
But I always say I didn't discover a soul.

1024
00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:19.500
They all came from these great managers and agents.

1025
00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:24.100
And then slowly improv comedy kind of got a presence in the comedy world.

1026
00:44:24.100 --> 00:44:26.100
And we would bring those people into audition.

1027
00:44:26.740 --> 00:44:27.460
Let's see.

1028
00:44:27.580 --> 00:44:31.560
We have a question here from Kimberly Ness from Rochester, Minnesota.

1029
00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:37.100
Writes in and says, Allison, what was your all-time favorite episode of The Office and why?

1030
00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:38.180
Wow.

1031
00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:43.560
I mean, it could be as a fan or from a casting perspective, I guess.

1032
00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:47.960
Well, it would be both my all-time favorite episode.

1033
00:44:48.140 --> 00:44:56.360
It probably, as a fan, was the basketball one when Stanley was bouncing the basketball and he couldn't do it, that episode.

1034
00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:57.840
And I think I went to the set that day.

1035
00:44:57.860 --> 00:44:58.980
So I saw a little bit of it.

1036
00:44:59.100 --> 00:44:59.620
So it was my favorite.

1037
00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:01.700
That had to have been my all-time favorite episode.

1038
00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:03.880
It's not everybody else's all-time favorite episode.

1039
00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:05.120
That's one of our all-time favorites.

1040
00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:05.500
Oh, it is?

1041
00:45:05.620 --> 00:45:06.920
That's a pretty great episode.

1042
00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:11.980
And also Phyllis' wedding for obvious reasons because it was Phyllis and she was so good.

1043
00:45:12.100 --> 00:45:14.680
And also Jim and Pam's wedding for sure.

1044
00:45:14.680 --> 00:45:18.060
And then you came dancing down the aisle like that YouTube video.

1045
00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:19.940
I think they took that from the YouTube video.

1046
00:45:20.140 --> 00:45:20.260
They did.

1047
00:45:20.540 --> 00:45:21.720
Yeah, so that was pretty cool.

1048
00:45:21.800 --> 00:45:23.780
I think those were my favorite episodes for sure.

1049
00:45:24.920 --> 00:45:33.660
Well, Allison, we just got so much mail for you because our fans just adore you and the work that you've done on creating The Office and so many of their favorite shows.

1050
00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:35.140
So I wanted to read you this.

1051
00:45:35.460 --> 00:45:37.040
This is from Brenna C.

1052
00:45:37.220 --> 00:45:37.920
in New York.

1053
00:45:38.240 --> 00:45:39.000
Hi, Allison.

1054
00:45:39.200 --> 00:45:44.460
Not a question, but just wanted to say how much of an impact you have had on the television industry

1055
00:45:44.460 --> 00:45:45.360
throughout the years.

1056
00:45:45.580 --> 00:45:53.120
It is so comforting and delightful to be watching a sitcom from a decade within the last 30 plus years and see your name pop up.

1057
00:45:53.580 --> 00:46:00.960
You have been directly responsible for the most beloved characters on our screens, and that does not go unnoticed by fans.

1058
00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:01.940
That's so nice.

1059
00:46:02.180 --> 00:46:05.180
Anyone who knows TV knows Allison Jones.

1060
00:46:05.620 --> 00:46:08.100
We see you and we appreciate you.

1061
00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:16.400
Thank you for taking chances on people who are trying to live their dreams because it makes dreams real for millions of people from that point on.

1062
00:46:16.940 --> 00:46:17.900
Thank you, Brenna.

1063
00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:19.140
Isn't that so lovely?

1064
00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:20.280
Yes, that's amazing.

1065
00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:22.080
Yes, thank you so much.

1066
00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:23.440
Yes.

1067
00:46:23.620 --> 00:46:25.420
And we got a lot of letters like that.

1068
00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:26.140
That's incredible.

1069
00:46:26.140 --> 00:46:38.620
I mean, for me, Allison, I was out here for eight years, and you were the casting director that kept calling me back.

1070
00:46:38.620 --> 00:46:44.960
I just needed you, really, to believe in me and give me that access to those parts.

1071
00:46:45.220 --> 00:46:45.400
Right.

1072
00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:49.900
And that is what changed my life, but it also kept me going.

1073
00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:57.280
I mean, just the fact that every six or seven months, Allison Jones would call with a part for me.

1074
00:46:57.520 --> 00:46:59.340
Maybe it was just a guest star.

1075
00:46:59.600 --> 00:47:00.900
Maybe it was a new pilot.

1076
00:47:01.660 --> 00:47:05.580
That was why I wasn't flying back home to St. Louis and giving up.

1077
00:47:05.700 --> 00:47:05.940
Yes, right, right.

1078
00:47:06.140 --> 00:47:09.520
Was because my phone would ring and you would bring me in.

1079
00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:09.980
Right.

1080
00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:12.020
And I so appreciate it.

1081
00:47:12.020 --> 00:47:13.000
Oh, you're so welcome.

1082
00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:16.880
And also, you're both so good at this, this podcast thing.

1083
00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:17.780
It's quite amazing.

1084
00:47:18.220 --> 00:47:18.960
Thank you.

1085
00:47:20.800 --> 00:47:26.800
But I would say that that is a large amount of faith that we have in people and in actors.

1086
00:47:26.980 --> 00:47:34.960
And when actors ask about what's the thing that you can advise actors on or what is the one word you have for actors, and I say tenacity.

1087
00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:38.280
Faith for the people who have to choose you.

1088
00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:41.580
Leap of faith for the people who have to choose you and for actors, tenacity.

1089
00:47:42.260 --> 00:47:43.460
So many actors.

1090
00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:45.200
God bless him.

1091
00:47:45.300 --> 00:47:45.700
Ken Jeong.

1092
00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:48.460
I brought him in so many times for Curb.

1093
00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:50.180
He finally got a Curb.

1094
00:47:50.320 --> 00:47:51.440
He finally got an office.

1095
00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:53.960
And now Ken is doing okay.

1096
00:47:54.740 --> 00:47:58.580
But yes, we have faith and you have to have the tenacity.

1097
00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:05.920
So, and that is the big story of many successful actors, I think, having the tenacity and the –

1098
00:48:06.540 --> 00:48:12.440
My acting coach, he used to say, success is opportunity meets readiness.

1099
00:48:12.720 --> 00:48:12.980
Yes.

1100
00:48:13.180 --> 00:48:13.420
Yes.

1101
00:48:13.420 --> 00:48:15.200
You can't control the opportunities.

1102
00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:15.580
Right.

1103
00:48:15.820 --> 00:48:17.320
But you can control the readiness.

1104
00:48:17.620 --> 00:48:17.760
Right.

1105
00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:22.220
So you just be ready for when Allison Jones calls, basically.

1106
00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:23.900
You're the opportunity piece.

1107
00:48:24.100 --> 00:48:24.440
Oh, good.

1108
00:48:24.520 --> 00:48:25.420
Thank you very much.

1109
00:48:25.420 --> 00:48:30.140
And, you know, I just tried to always be ready.

1110
00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:35.320
And he also said the key is building a consistent body of work.

1111
00:48:35.380 --> 00:48:35.620
Yes.

1112
00:48:35.800 --> 00:48:41.080
So that every time – and he would literally use your name because you were the casting director that was calling.

1113
00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:47.780
He would say every time Allison calls, you are ready and you show her a consistent body of work.

1114
00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:54.100
And if you do those two things, eventually, eventually, the right fit will find you.

1115
00:48:54.480 --> 00:49:03.040
My first boss to whom I owe everything, the late Judith Wiener – anyway, I owe her everything in terms of any kind of casting theory.

1116
00:49:03.500 --> 00:49:07.220
But she used to always say there is one perfect part for every actor.

1117
00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:08.720
So – and I believe that.

1118
00:49:08.960 --> 00:49:11.300
She said there is one perfect part for every actor.

1119
00:49:11.440 --> 00:49:11.800
Well –

1120
00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:12.920
And keep bringing them in.

1121
00:49:13.100 --> 00:49:13.320
Yeah.

1122
00:49:13.580 --> 00:49:15.500
Thank you for fighting for us because –

1123
00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:16.020
Oh, please.

1124
00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:17.360
We know you did.

1125
00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:18.280
Yeah.

1126
00:49:18.500 --> 00:49:19.500
We do all the time.

1127
00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:21.680
We know we wouldn't be here without you, Allison.

1128
00:49:21.920 --> 00:49:22.240
Truly.

1129
00:49:22.480 --> 00:49:24.060
It means so much to us.

1130
00:49:24.300 --> 00:49:24.620
Really.

1131
00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:24.840
Yeah.

1132
00:49:24.960 --> 00:49:25.140
I'll get –

1133
00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:25.820
Thank you for this.

1134
00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:26.220
Choked up.

1135
00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:26.560
Yeah.

1136
00:49:26.640 --> 00:49:27.340
No, I would too.

1137
00:49:27.460 --> 00:49:27.560
Yeah.

1138
00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:28.500
You changed our lives.

1139
00:49:29.380 --> 00:49:37.480
After I came back to the office after interviewing with Greg and everybody, I remember saying to Phyllis, the before times Phyllis, God, I hope I get this job.

1140
00:49:37.580 --> 00:49:39.860
I think it would be one of the most important jobs I've ever had.

1141
00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.500
This is – this show is fantastic.

1142
00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:43.000
It's the office, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

1143
00:49:43.580 --> 00:49:47.020
And little did I know it was more important for Phyllis.

1144
00:49:49.460 --> 00:49:49.940
Yeah.

1145
00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:54.160
Hugely life-changing and important for me, but at the time, who knew?

1146
00:49:54.500 --> 00:49:54.660
Yeah.

1147
00:49:54.820 --> 00:49:55.060
Yeah.

1148
00:49:55.220 --> 00:49:55.820
Who knew?

1149
00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:56.160
Who knew?

1150
00:49:56.160 --> 00:49:58.100
It was really important for Phyllis.

1151
00:49:58.240 --> 00:49:59.020
So amazing.

1152
00:49:59.020 --> 00:50:01.360
And for the people who make Inside Out.

1153
00:50:01.780 --> 00:50:02.060
Yeah.

1154
00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:03.860
How proud are we of Phyllis?

1155
00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:04.720
She's amazing.

1156
00:50:04.860 --> 00:50:05.020
So amazing.

1157
00:50:05.020 --> 00:50:05.620
I was crying.

1158
00:50:05.740 --> 00:50:06.720
Yeah, that you just said.

1159
00:50:08.320 --> 00:50:09.140
Hi, Phyllis.

1160
00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:10.380
So happy for Phyllis.

1161
00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:11.440
So happy for Phyllis.

1162
00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:14.980
Well, Allison, this was absolutely delightful.

1163
00:50:15.480 --> 00:50:22.440
Is there any memory or anything that we have not touched on that you'd like to share with our listeners?

1164
00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:24.760
I'm going to look through our questions one more time.

1165
00:50:24.760 --> 00:50:25.780
Yes, I would like to share.

1166
00:50:25.880 --> 00:50:27.420
You had a question there that you didn't ask.

1167
00:50:27.560 --> 00:50:27.840
Oh.

1168
00:50:27.960 --> 00:50:33.860
You had asked on the questions something about a special phone call.

1169
00:50:34.220 --> 00:50:34.780
Oh, yes.

1170
00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:35.400
Here it is.

1171
00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:35.660
Read that.

1172
00:50:35.700 --> 00:50:36.160
Oh, yeah, yeah.

1173
00:50:36.380 --> 00:50:36.720
Yes.

1174
00:50:37.140 --> 00:50:38.300
This was my question.

1175
00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:38.600
Yes.

1176
00:50:38.600 --> 00:50:39.460
This is from Jenna F.

1177
00:50:39.600 --> 00:50:40.120
in Los Angeles.

1178
00:50:40.120 --> 00:50:41.300
Oh, Jenna F.

1179
00:50:41.540 --> 00:50:42.000
writes in sometimes.

1180
00:50:42.020 --> 00:50:42.540
Okay, Jenna.

1181
00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:42.860
Yeah.

1182
00:50:43.100 --> 00:50:43.200
Okay.

1183
00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:52.960
Jenna said, I'm sure it's great to call and tell someone that they got a role, but you have to make a dozen other calls to tell all the other people that they didn't get the role.

1184
00:50:53.240 --> 00:50:54.540
And what's that like?

1185
00:50:55.760 --> 00:50:59.780
Telling the actors they got the roles is the job, but mostly the managers and the agents.

1186
00:50:59.940 --> 00:51:04.800
So we have to call and tell the agents and the managers that their client did or didn't get the role.

1187
00:51:05.660 --> 00:51:08.220
And the one I remember the most is you, Jenna Fisher.

1188
00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:09.160
What?

1189
00:51:09.160 --> 00:51:10.140
And you got the office.

1190
00:51:10.340 --> 00:51:10.600
Okay.

1191
00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:20.100
I probably, the worst call was having to tell Bob Odenkirk he didn't get the office, but I was able to tell the people who got the office, they got the office.

1192
00:51:20.200 --> 00:51:21.320
So I called up Michael Green.

1193
00:51:21.740 --> 00:51:23.120
That was my agent at the time.

1194
00:51:23.320 --> 00:51:24.680
Your agent at the time, Michael Green.

1195
00:51:24.920 --> 00:51:28.100
And they always know what you're calling about right after a teSt.

1196
00:51:28.600 --> 00:51:29.920
And I said, hi, Michael.

1197
00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:36.140
And the import of it hit me as much as it probably hit him and would have hit you.

1198
00:51:36.140 --> 00:51:42.200
I said, I am calling to tell you that Jenna Fisher got the office.

1199
00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:44.860
It's exactly what I said.

1200
00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:45.880
And there was silence.

1201
00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:46.600
There was silence.

1202
00:51:46.780 --> 00:51:48.600
I think he was probably crying a little bit.

1203
00:51:48.860 --> 00:51:50.320
I was even getting emotional.

1204
00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:59.640
Because for some reason at the time, it hit me that that was a big deal for Jenna Fisher to get the office.

1205
00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:01.140
It was a big deal for me.

1206
00:52:01.140 --> 00:52:04.100
And who even knew at the time what would happen with the office?

1207
00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:04.460
Yeah.

1208
00:52:04.760 --> 00:52:05.000
You know.

1209
00:52:05.680 --> 00:52:07.760
So that is the one that I remember the moSt.

1210
00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:09.220
I hate having to call.

1211
00:52:09.340 --> 00:52:10.300
I hate making those calls.

1212
00:52:10.540 --> 00:52:11.820
It falls on us, of course.

1213
00:52:11.960 --> 00:52:12.360
God forbid.

1214
00:52:12.680 --> 00:52:15.760
Over the years, I've had to call and actually fire actors from a set.

1215
00:52:15.920 --> 00:52:16.540
It's awful.

1216
00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:17.300
No, it's awful.

1217
00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:20.600
Because people don't man up and do it themselves.

1218
00:52:20.860 --> 00:52:21.880
You know, directors and things.

1219
00:52:21.980 --> 00:52:23.020
They don't want to do that kind of thing.

1220
00:52:23.080 --> 00:52:24.280
So we get to do the dirty work.

1221
00:52:24.880 --> 00:52:34.960
So what it's like is it's awful, but it's great to be able to tell an agent or a manager that their client got a huge role that we know will be life-changing.

1222
00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:39.320
And even the best are the small roles that will be life-changing.

1223
00:52:39.440 --> 00:52:47.680
When I get to hire somebody phenomenal for one line on Curb Your Enthusiasm, and they're the ones who send bottles of wine and flowers.

1224
00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:50.860
It's like, yeah, you're spending your whole salary and it's one line.

1225
00:52:50.980 --> 00:52:53.100
But to them, it's the start of everything.

1226
00:52:53.100 --> 00:52:59.420
So that's what's the greatest part of casting is getting these people you believe in, finally getting them the gigs.

1227
00:52:59.960 --> 00:53:01.860
And it takes so long.

1228
00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:04.760
Jack Black used to come in for one-line parts all the time.

1229
00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:05.920
And he killed it.

1230
00:53:06.120 --> 00:53:08.380
And talk about a person who could kill one line.

1231
00:53:08.480 --> 00:53:09.060
It was Jack Black.

1232
00:53:09.360 --> 00:53:10.500
I could kill in a good way.

1233
00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:14.200
And when he finally started getting the parts, it was phenomenal.

1234
00:53:14.600 --> 00:53:18.740
You know, the rest is history for Jack Black, but still probably a full decade of Jack Black.

1235
00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:21.640
But anyway, it's the small parts that are pretty amazing, too.

1236
00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:23.300
And the unknown people to get a part.

1237
00:53:23.500 --> 00:53:26.260
You know that it's a joyful, wonderful thing.

1238
00:53:26.640 --> 00:53:27.680
Allison, this was amazing.

1239
00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:29.860
Thank you so much for coming in.

1240
00:53:30.100 --> 00:53:31.780
Yes, Allison, thank you.

1241
00:53:32.080 --> 00:53:36.220
You had such a deeply impactful role in casting The Office.

1242
00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:40.420
And I'm just so excited to share this interview with our fans.

1243
00:53:44.390 --> 00:53:46.970
Well, I just loved that so much.

